  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nRain continues across the area this evening. The sfc low will \ncontinue to track NE across the region while the midlevel low slowly\ntrudges NE through the srn OH Vly and nrn TN Vly.\n\nThermal profiles will remain too warm locally through midnight to \nsupport any mixing of rain with snow, but as the deeper-layer low \ncenters pivot to our E during the predawn hours, the wind profile \nwill become more westerly, supporting CAA within a deeper layer of \nthe column. This will steepen lapse rates and usher in perhaps just \nenough cold air to support some mixing of snow with the rain early \nFriday morning. As we progress toward daybreak and beyond, drier air \nwithin the DGZ will become more pronounced, with the once very-deep \nmoisture becoming increasingly shallow through the first part of the \ndaytime. This, alongside less forcing, will yield an overall \nweakening/lightening of the banded precip into the afternoon. \nHowever, sporadic light banded precip may continue through the \nentire daytime before nudging to the E of the local area by sunset \nFriday evening. Cannot completely rule out a dusting of snow \naccumulation on grassy surfaces (mainly near/W of I-75 into ern IN) \nearly morning if there are brief periods where the rain transitions \ncompletely to snow. But minimal impacts are expected given above \nfreezing air temps and antecedent (very) warm ground conditions. Any \nvery minor grassy/elevated accumulation will melt quickly into late \nmorning and early afternoon.\n\nAs the elongated sfc low (with a trailing sfc trof) pivots N into \nthe ern OH Vly past 03z, winds should temporarily subside as the \npressure gradient briefly relaxes through about 12z. However, after \ndaybreak, the sfc pressure gradient should begin to tighten again. \nThis, alongside increasing CAA through the column (steepening LL \nlapse rates), will support a gradual increase in sustained winds and \ngustiness into Friday afternoon continuing through the evening. \nWesterly winds of 15-20kts, with gusts to around 25-30kts, can be \nexpected from about 20z-04z before gustiness tapers off to 20kts or \nless toward midnight and beyond.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n \nBrief midlevel ridging will build into the region Friday night into\nearly Saturday before flow becomes more zonal once again by Saturday\nnight. This will provide some tranquil weather across the OH Vly\nbefore a digging trof and quick-moving system comes back in by early\nSunday. A strengthening system will translate into the mid MS/OH \nVlys by early Sunday, promoting sfc cyclogenesis/deepening as the low\nmoves toward the ILN FA Sunday morning.\n\nWidespread rain will overspread the area by late morning into the\nafternoon as the dynamic system moves through the OH Vly. Despite a\ndeepening system and corresponding enhancement of the deep-layer and\nLL wind fields, instby should remain very limited as sfc dewpoints\nwill struggle to reach into the lower 50s, even in N KY. As the sfc\nlow pulls east quickly Sunday evening into Sunday night, gusty winds\nowing to a tightening pressure gradient/rapid height rises and\nCAA/steepening lapse rates, will overspread the area. Gusts of 30-35\nMPH are expected Sunday evening/night before relaxing into the\npredawn hours as a sfc high builds in from the west for the daytime\nMonday. \n\nCooler air will filter in for Monday, but drier conditions and a \nwarming trend are expected heading into the middle of next week.",
